<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>A German citizen working in Singapore and an Indian lawyer practising in Delhi, never married to each other, have a son born on 15 July 2022. When the relationship broke down, the mother filed a habeas corpus petition before the Bombay High Court (Goa Bench) in February 2026; the father filed a guardianship petition in Delhi. By the time the matter reached the Supreme Court under Article 136, both parents had been assessed at the Court&rsquo;s instance by NIMHANS, which found each of them capable and emotionally invested in the child.</p>

<h2>Jurisdiction yields to welfare</h2>
<p>Two threshold objections framed the appeal: that the habeas petition had become infructuous once a guardianship court was seized of the matter, and that international elements complicated the Indian courts&rsquo; authority. The Court rejected both, exercising parens patriae jurisdiction — the constitutional court&rsquo;s protective role over those who cannot protect themselves. It held that <em>&ldquo;irrespective of the nature of the proceeding before a court, when the custody right over a child is involved, the child&rsquo;s best interests must be the preeminent consideration; it must prevail over all other considerations&rdquo;</em> — and that <em>&ldquo;even international jurisdictional issues cannot come in the way of the best interests of the child.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>The operative scheme</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Three weeks for a mirror order</strong> — the mother is to obtain an order from a Singapore court reproducing the Supreme Court&rsquo;s conditions, making the arrangement enforceable abroad.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Rs. 1 crore deposit</strong> — thereafter she deposits Rs. 1 crore with the Registry, held for two years or until the Delhi guardianship petition is decided.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Interim schedule in Delhi</strong> — pending the mirror order the child remains in Delhi on a weekly split: Monday to Friday with the mother, weekends with the father; whenever the mother is not in Delhi, the child stays with the father.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Travel documents on compliance</strong> — once the mirror order is filed, the Registry returns the child&rsquo;s passport and OCI card and the mother may take him to Singapore, subject to appearing when directed.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the judgment matters for practitioners</h2>
<p>Cross-border custody disputes sit at the junction of habeas corpus, guardianship law and private international law, and clients frequently receive conflicting advice about which door to knock. This decision confirms that the Supreme Court will not let the choice of door decide the child&rsquo;s fate: the writ survives parallel guardianship proceedings, and interim custody is settled on the familiar triad — prima facie case, balance of convenience, and the child&rsquo;s welfare as the paramount consideration. For the parent remaining in India, the architecture of safeguards is the real takeaway: mirror orders, financial deposits, document custody with the Registry, and preserved guardianship proceedings keep the foreign-resident parent answerable to the Indian court.</p>
<div class="note">The judgment settles interim custody only; the father&rsquo;s guardianship petition remains pending, and the Court&rsquo;s observations do not prejudge it. Families in similar situations should note that each element of the scheme — the mirror order, the deposit, the schedule — was tailored to this record, and arrangements in other cases will differ with the child&rsquo;s age, roots and circumstances.</div>
